          "misconception": "quantity crossed"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1::Q6",
      "moduleId": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1",
      "stem": "State the overall enthalpy change in terms of bond breaking and formation.",
      "misconceptionClass": "master recall",
      "kind": "recall",
      "modelAnswer": "The overall enthalpy change equals the total energy required to break the bonds minus the total energy released when the new bonds form.",
      "keywords": [
        "break",
        "form",
        "minus"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1::Q7",
      "moduleId": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1",
      "stem": "Classify bond breaking and bond formation as endothermic or exothermic.",
      "misconceptionClass": "direction recall",
      "kind": "recall",
      "modelAnswer": "Bond breaking is endothermic (energy absorbed); bond formation is exothermic (energy released).",
      "keywords": [
        "breaking",
        "endothermic",
        "formation"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1::Q8",
      "moduleId": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1",
      "stem": "Explain why bond breaking always costs energy.",
      "misconceptionClass": "cost reasoning",
      "kind": "explain-why",
      "modelAnswer": "A bond exists because the paired electrons sit at lower energy between the two nuclei; pulling the atoms apart must lift the pair back to the higher, free-atom state, and that lift is paid from the surroundings.",
      "keywords": [
        "lower energy",
        "pull",
        "surroundings"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1::Q9",
      "moduleId": "rn_GpHSrcx5SvbCXDG3::L1",
      "stem": "A reaction's ledger shows 520 kJ of breaking against 700 kJ of formation. State the overall enthalpy change and its class.",
      "misconceptionClass": "ledger application",
      "kind": "application",
      "modelAnswer": "The balance is 520 - 700 = -180 kJ mol-1, exothermic, because formation outearned breaking.",
      "keywords": [
        "-180",
        "exothermic",
        "formation"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
